diagnosis_text:
I reproduced the user’s path from 172.16.20.x to 172.16.10.x and found inter‑VLAN connectivity working as designed. From the affected segment (laptop0 at 172.16.20.10), ICMP to 172.16.10.4 (PC4) and 172.16.10.5 succeeds with 0% loss and low latency (e.g., 1.1–1.5 ms, TTL=63 indicating one routed hop). The default gateway 172.16.20.1 and the opposite SVI 172.16.10.1 on router0 also respond normally. An iperf test from laptop0 to PC4 sustained 60–80 Mbit/s with zero retransmits, showing stable L3 forwarding. 

On the routing device, router0 has the expected router-on-a-stick setup: VLAN subinterfaces eth0.20 (172.16.20.1/24) and eth0.10 (172.16.10.1/24) are up, and its routing table shows both networks directly connected. Other hosts (e.g., pc11/pc12/pc13/pc14) can also reach PC4 across the network without loss, further confirming end-to-end health.

Localization: Inter‑VLAN routing between VLAN 20 and VLAN 10 via router0’s eth0.{20,10} subinterfaces and the mswitch1/switch1 trunk is operating correctly. No switchport, interface, or routing daemon fault is observed.

Root cause: Not reproducible; the network path is currently healthy. The earlier symptom may have been transient or application/endpoint-related (e.g., host firewall/service, name resolution).

Corrective fix: No network changes required. Ask the user to retry by IP (e.g., ping 172.16.10.4). If issues persist, verify endpoint firewalls/services on targets (printers/PC4) and local name/DNS resolution on the laptop; capture timestamps to investigate any intermittent events. 
